aturday 10 October at 12:00.","The research block was rewritten and dated 26 September. All ten gameweek 6 match prices were refreshed and gameweek 7 now carries all ten as well, up from three, so the second week of the plan is priced rather than assumed. Six start probabilities are set ahead of the official flag: Semenyo and Brobbey down, van Ewijk and Dunk up after each missed a single match, Havertz down, and João Pedro held. A note warns that there are two players called Hughes: you own the fit Crystal Palace midfielder, not the injured Hull defender.","The model was re-scored on the last three gameweeks using only what was known at the time. It ranks points better than a simple average, 0.158 against 0.098, and predicts who starts better than 'same as last week', 0.131 against 0.148 where lower is better. It has no edge at all on team goals, so that part drives nothing. It is still under-forecasting goalkeepers, defenders and midfielders, so those carry corrections of 12, 8 and 5 per cent; forwards are unchanged.","The Classic plan was solved again on an idle machine for five more minutes, which closed the proof: it is now within 1 per cent of the best plan that exists, where the first pass was 6 per cent away. Keeping the wildcard unplayed was solved to the exact best, so the comparison has a floor rather than an estimate: a fresh fifteen in gameweek 6 is worth about 12 expected points more than holding the chip, and that direction is proved. The plan also puts Bench Boost in gameweek 7 and Triple Captain in gameweek 8. Which week the wildcard belongs in is the closer call, and the timing panel says exactly how close.","The Draft claims were re-solved against the pool as it stands. All eight swaps are like for like, the roster they leave you with is a legal 2-5-5-3, and they are ordered by what each is worth once the chance of it landing is counted, with one team claiming ahead of you in every round.","Five sentences that had numbers typed into them now read t